# XSH cacosh(), cacoshf() and cacoshl()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Only what the page makes exact is tested: special values (signed zeros,
# infinities, NaN parts), the symmetry rules and the exact results it states.
# The accuracy of other results is not tested. Arguments are built with
# CMPLX() and pass through volatile objects, and the three types (name, name
# with f, name with l) are each tested. The special values on this page carry
# the tag for the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point option and are NOTINUSE
# where it is not claimed.

assertion  XSH.cacosh.001
anchor     functions/cacosh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       cacosh of +0 + i0 and of -0 + i0 has real part +0.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/001-zero.c

assertion  XSH.cacosh.002
anchor     functions/cacosh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      disputed
says       The imaginary part of cacosh(+-0 + i0) is pi/2.
note       disputed (Q-111): the page names the numbers pi, pi/2, pi/4 and
           3pi/4, which no binary type holds, and does not say the value is
           the nearest representable one. The test expects the decimal value
           rounded to the type. The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559
           Complex Floating-Point option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C
           implementation claims it (Annex G of the C standard). No error is
           defined on this page, so errno is not examined; an exception is
           checked only when math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/002-zero-pi.c

assertion  XSH.cacosh.003
anchor     functions/cacosh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       cacosh of x + iInf (x finite, including zeros) has real part +Inf.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/003-finite-inf-imag.c

assertion  XSH.cacosh.004
anchor     functions/cacosh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      disputed
says       The imaginary part of cacosh(x + iInf) (x finite, including zeros)
           is pi/2.
note       disputed (Q-111): the page names the numbers pi, pi/2, pi/4 and
           3pi/4, which no binary type holds, and does not say the value is
           the nearest representable one. The test expects the decimal value
           rounded to the type. The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559
           Complex Floating-Point option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C
           implementation claims it (Annex G of the C standard). No error is
           defined on this page, so errno is not examined; an exception is
           checked only when math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/004-finite-inf-imag-pi.c

assertion  XSH.cacosh.005
anchor     functions/cacosh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       cacosh of +0 + iNaN has a NaN real part.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT. The page's wording leaves
           open which zeros, signs or infinities the row covers; only +0,
           positive values and +Inf are used. See Q-114.
test       t/005-zero-nan.c

assertion  XSH.cacosh.006
anchor     functions/cacosh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      disputed
says       The imaginary part of cacosh(+0 + iNaN) is pi/2 or -pi/2.
note       disputed (Q-111): the page names the numbers pi, pi/2, pi/4 and
           3pi/4, which no binary type holds, and does not say the value is
           the nearest representable one. The test expects the decimal value
           rounded to the type. The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559
           Complex Floating-Point option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C
           implementation claims it (Annex G of the C standard). No error is
           defined on this page, so errno is not examined; an exception is
           checked only when math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT. The
           page's wording leaves open which zeros, signs or infinities the row
           covers; only +0, positive values and +Inf are used. See Q-114.
test       t/006-zero-nan-pi.c

assertion  XSH.cacosh.007
anchor     functions/cacosh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       cacosh of x + iNaN (x non-zero finite), of NaN + iy (y finite,
           including zeros) and of NaN + iNaN is NaN + iNaN.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/007-nan.c

assertion  XSH.cacosh.008
anchor     functions/cacosh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       cacosh of -Inf + iy (y positive finite: 1, 2.5) has real part +Inf.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/008-minus-inf-finite.c

assertion  XSH.cacosh.009
anchor     functions/cacosh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      disputed
says       The imaginary part of cacosh(-Inf + iy) (y positive finite: 1, 2.5)
           is pi.
note       disputed (Q-111): the page names the numbers pi, pi/2, pi/4 and
           3pi/4, which no binary type holds, and does not say the value is
           the nearest representable one. The test expects the decimal value
           rounded to the type. The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559
           Complex Floating-Point option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C
           implementation claims it (Annex G of the C standard). No error is
           defined on this page, so errno is not examined; an exception is
           checked only when math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/009-minus-inf-finite-pi.c

assertion  XSH.cacosh.010
anchor     functions/cacosh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       cacosh of +Inf + iy (y positive finite: 1, 2.5) is +Inf + i0.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
